  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 1211 AM MST Sat Nov 28 2026/ \n\nModerate (0.5\" to 1.0\" per hour) to at times heavy (1.0\" to 1.5\" per \nhour) snowfall will persist through the remainder of the overnight \nhours across Southwest Montana, with generally light snowfall \nextending as far north as the Island Ranges of Central Montana as a \nshortwave embedded within northwest flow aloft moves over the \nNorthern Rockies. Snowfall rates will decrease rapidly near sunrise \nthis morning, with generally light snow lingering over the Madison \nand Gallatin ranges through the early afternoon hours before \nending. As snowfall rates decrease through sunrise this morning \ntemperatures will fall into the 20s, which combined with wet \nsurfaces will support the opportunity for a flash freeze any many \nroadways across Southwest Montana. This flash freeze will make \ntravel hazardous through the remainder of the morning, with \ngradually improving conditions through the noon hour as skies clear \nand temperatures warm to near freezing.\n\nOtherwise a relatively benign pattern is expected through the \nremainder of the weekend as upper level ridging builds in over the \nNorthern Rockies through this timeframe. This upper level ridge will \nridge will help to keep temperatures near to slightly above normal \nthrough Sunday, with overall dry conditions and light winds winds \nexpected. The exception to these light winds will be in the north-\nsouth orientated valleys of Southwest Montana where southerly winds \nwill be breezy and gusty, most notably in the Madison River Valley \nwhere there is a 30% chance that wind gusts exceed 40 mph on Sunday.\n\nThe upcoming work week looks to be active as the upper level flow \nbecomes more zonal in nature, with several shortwaves rippling \nthrough and over the Northern Rockies. While high winds are not \nexpected at this time across Southwest through North Central \nMontana, southwest to west surface winds will be strong and gusty at \ntimes along the Rocky Mountain Front and across the Montana Hwy \n200/US Hwy 87 corridors; particularly from Tuesday through Friday. \nTemperatures will moderate over the plains thanks to these gusty \nwinds, with the valleys of Southwest and Central Montana remaining \nnear to slightly below normal thanks to strong inversions setting up \nover the valley floors. Several opportunities for mainly mountain \nprecipitation will arrive through the work week thanks to the \nshortwaves rippling through the zonal flow, with the best \nopportunity arriving Monday morning and lasting through Monday \nnight. - Moldan",
